       figured with CCOONNFFIIGG__KKEEXXEECC.

   kkeexxeecc__ffiillee__llooaadd(())
       The kkeexxeecc__ffiillee__llooaadd() system call is similar to  kkeexxeecc__llooaadd(),  but  it
       takes  a  different set of arguments.  It reads the kernel to be loaded
       from the file referred to by the file  descriptor  _k_e_r_n_e_l___f_d,  and  the
       initrd  (initial  RAM  disk)  to be loaded from file referred to by the
       file descriptor _i_n_i_t_r_d___f_d.  The _c_m_d_l_i_n_e argument  is  a  pointer  to  a
       buffer containing the command line for the new kernel.  The _c_m_d_l_i_n_e___l_e_n
       argument  specifies  size  of  the buffer.  The last byte in the buffer
       must be a null byte ('\0').

       The _f_l_a_g_s argument is a bit mask which modifies  the  behavior  of  the
       call.  The following values can be specified in _f_l_a_g_s:

       KKEEXXEECC__FFIILLEE__UUNNLLOOAADD
              Unload the currently loaded kernel.

       KKEEXXEECC__FFIILLEE__OONN__CCRRAASSHH
              Load  the new kernel in the memory region reserved for the crash
              kernel (as for KKEEXXEECC__OONN__CCRRAASSHH).  This kernel is  booted  if  the
              currently running kernel crashes.

       KKEEXXEECC__FFIILLEE__NNOO__IINNIITTRRAAMMFFSS
              Loading  initrd/initramfs  is optional.  Specify this flag if no
